          "your eyes or your hands for the steps that ask you to read, point, write or gesture"
        ],
        "waitUntilStopped": [
          "Grammar Lens: direct owner",
          "Guided Practice"
        ],
        "text": "Before we start: this one needs your eyes, so it is not fully a driving lesson. You will want your eyes or your hands for the steps that ask you to read, point, write or gesture. You can listen to everything else now. I will say so again when we reach them, but leave these sections until you have stopped: Grammar Lens: direct owner and Guided Practice."
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Recall the practice name ਅਮਨ, “work” ਕੰਮ, and the completed form ਕੀਤਾ."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: direct owner",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ਨੇ — ne — marks the person who did the action in this frame ਅਮਨ ਨੇ ਕੰਮ ਕੀਤਾ — Aman ne kamm kiitaa — Aman did the work."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Put ਨੇ (ne) immediately after ਅਮਨ in this one third-person completed transitive action. The short marker points to the doer. Familiar ਕੀਤਾ still agrees with the unmarked masculine-singular object ਕੰਮ. Say the whole sentence once, then point to its doer, work, and action."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: source boundary",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Punjabi University Patiala's lesson 14 shows a comparable completed-action pattern and warns that usage varies by sentence. This is a safe small example; not every past sentence takes ਨੇ (ne). The same written sound can also be a present-tense auxiliary at the end of a different sentence; that is not today's marker. We do not generalise beyond this named-agent, third-person frame."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "HEAR",
              "instruction": "Aman ne kamm kiitaa",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU HEAR: *Aman ne kamm kiitaa*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say ਅਮਨ ਨੇ ਕੰਮ ਕੀਤਾ (Aman ne kamm kiitaa)."
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "READ",
              "instruction": "ਅਮਨ ਨੇ ਕੰਮ ਕੀਤਾ (Aman ne kamm kiitaa)",
              "spoken": false,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU READ: **ਅਮਨ ਨੇ ਕੰਮ ਕੀਤਾ**]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "WRITE",
              "instruction": "copy only ਨੇ (ne), checking ਨ then ੇ",
              "spoken": false,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU WRITE: copy only **ਨੇ**, checking **ਨ** then **ੇ**]"
            }
          ]
        },
